opengl32: Graceful fallback when Vulkan placed mapping
 is not honored

When a 32-bit GL/D3D application maps a buffer, opengl32's WOW64 layer
tries to use VK_MEMORY_MAP_PLACED_BIT_EXT to map the Vulkan backing
memory at a 32-bit-addressable virtual address (vm_ptr). If the Vulkan
driver honours the requested placement, the host_ptr returned by
vkMapMemory2KHR equals vm_ptr and the buffer can be exposed to the
32-bit app directly (zero-copy).

The original code asserted that the placement succeeded:

    assert( buffer->host_ptr == buffer->vm_ptr );

Unfortunately, some Vulkan drivers/wrappers advertise
VK_MEMORY_MAP_PLACED_BIT_EXT in their feature bits but don't actually
honour the requested placement -- notably the Turnip wrapper used by
WinHub on Termux-X11. With those drivers, vkMapMemory2KHR returns a
host_ptr that is NOT equal to vm_ptr, the assertion fires, and every
32-bit GL/D3D application that maps a buffer (e.g. any D3D7 app that
uploads vertex buffers via wined3d) crashes with an SEH exception
"Exception frame is not in stack limits".

This patch replaces the assertion with a graceful fallback:

  * If host_ptr == vm_ptr, behave as before (zero-copy placed mapping).
  * Otherwise, emit a one-time FIXME and fall back to the same
    copy-based path already used for non-pinned buffers:
      - Keep the Vulkan mapping (host_ptr) for GPU access.
      - Expose vm_ptr (32-bit-addressable) to the 32-bit application.
      - Set buffer->copy_length so that wglUnmapBuffer copies the
        application's writes back from vm_ptr to host_ptr before
        unmapping the Vulkan memory.
      - On map, copy the initial contents from host_ptr to vm_ptr
        unless the access flags invalidate the range/buffer.

This is slower than the zero-copy placed mapping, but it is correct
and matches what Wine already does for the non-Vulkan-pinned case.

Verified to fix the crash observed in 32-bit Direct3D 7 test apps on
Hangover Wine 11.14 + WinHub Turnip wrapper + Termux-X11 (zink).
64-bit apps are unaffected (they don't go through wow64_map_buffer).

diff --git a/dlls/opengl32/unix_wgl.c b/dlls/opengl32/unix_wgl.c
index 0ba0282..d762486 100644
--- a/dlls/opengl32/unix_wgl.c
+++ b/dlls/opengl32/unix_wgl.c
@@ -2079,9 +2079,41 @@ static void *wow64_map_buffer( TEB *teb, struct buffer *buffer, GLenum target, G
             ERR( "vkMapMemory2KHR failed: %d\n", vr );
             return NULL;
         }
-        assert( buffer->host_ptr == buffer->vm_ptr );
+        if (buffer->host_ptr == buffer->vm_ptr)
+        {
+            /* Placed mapping worked: Vulkan honoured our requested address. */
+            buffer->map_ptr = (char *)buffer->vm_ptr + offset;
+            TRACE( "returning vk mapping %p\n", buffer->map_ptr );
+            return buffer->map_ptr;
+        }
+
+        /* Placed mapping was NOT honoured by the driver (host_ptr != vm_ptr).
+         * This happens on some Vulkan drivers/wrappers that don't actually
+         * support VK_MEMORY_MAP_PLACED_BIT_EXT despite advertising it, e.g.
+         * the Turnip wrapper used by WinHub on Termux-X11. The original code
+         * asserted here, which crashes every 32-bit GL/D3D app that maps a
+         * buffer (e.g. D3D7 vertex buffers via wined3d).
+         *
+         * Fall back to the same copy-based path used for non-pinned buffers:
+         * keep the Vulkan mapping (host_ptr) for GPU access, expose vm_ptr
+         * to the 32-bit app, and copy back on unmap. Slower, but correct. */
+        {
+            static int once;
+            if (!once++)
+                FIXME( "VK_MEMORY_MAP_PLACED_BIT_EXT not honoured by driver "
+                       "(host %p != vm %p), using copy fallback.\n",
+                       buffer->host_ptr, buffer->vm_ptr );
+        }
+        if (!offset && !length) length = buffer->size;
         buffer->map_ptr = (char *)buffer->vm_ptr + offset;
-        TRACE( "returning vk mapping %p\n", buffer->map_ptr );
+        buffer->copy_length = (access & GL_MAP_WRITE_BIT) ? length : 0;
+        if (!(access & (GL_MAP_INVALIDATE_RANGE_BIT | GL_MAP_INVALIDATE_BUFFER_BIT)))
+        {
+            TRACE( "Copying %#zx from vk host %p to wow64 buffer %p\n",
+                   length, buffer->host_ptr, buffer->map_ptr );
+            memcpy( buffer->map_ptr, buffer->host_ptr, length );
+        }
+        TRACE( "returning copy buffer %p\n", buffer->map_ptr );
         return buffer->map_ptr;
     }
